findings.suggestion:
Replace `forEach(async ...)` with a construct that captures and awaits the promises. Push the deleteEvent PROMISES (not awaited results) into `apiDeletes`, e.g.:
```
for (const credential of bookingToDelete.user.credentials.filter((c) => c.type.endsWith("_calendar"))) {
  const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
  for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
    const bookingRef = updBooking.references.find((ref) => ref.type.includes("_calendar"));
    if (bookingRef) {
      const { uid, externalCalendarId } = bookingRef;
      apiDeletes.push(calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId) as Promise<unknown>);
    }
  }
}
```
This mirrors the legacy for...of branch at lines 480-483, ensures `apiDeletes` contains pending promises that `await apiDeletes` / `Promise.all(...)` will actually wait on, and lets getCalendar rejections propagate to the handler's error path. Alternatively, collect the forEach promises into a separate array and `await Promise.all(...)` it before returning.

Replace `forEach(async ...)` with an awaited `Promise.all` (or sequential `for...of` if ordering matters):

```ts
await Promise.all(bookingRefsFiltered.map(async (bookingRef) => {
  if (bookingRef.uid) {
    if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_calendar")) {
      const calendar = await getCalendar(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type));
      return calendar?.deleteEvent(
        bookingRef.uid,
        builder.calendarEvent,
        bookingRef.externalCalendarId
      );
    } else if (bookingRef.type.endsWith("_video")) {
      return deleteMeeting(credentialsMap.get(bookingRef.type), bookingRef.uid);
    }
  }
}));
```

This makes deletions awaitable (so the reschedule flow proceeds only after old events are removed) and surfaces rejection errors. Wrap individual deletions in `.catch(...)` if you want best-effort deletion that doesn't block the flow, but at least log failures.

Wrap the dynamic import lookup in a try/catch and degrade gracefully — at minimum, log the chunk-load failure distinctly so operators can distinguish it from a real PaymentService error:

```ts
let paymentApp;
try {
  paymentApp = await appStore[paymentAppCredential?.app?.dirName as keyof typeof appStore];
} catch (err) {
  console.error(`Failed to load payment app chunk ${paymentAppCredential?.app?.dirName}:`, err);
  throw new Error(`Payment app '${paymentAppCredential?.app?.dirName}' could not be loaded; refund aborted.`);
}
```

This gives operators an actionable error message and makes the failure mode traceable in logs. Apply the same pattern at the other `await appStore[key]` call sites added by this PR.
